## Observed failure

The native log has exactly three selector sections. Each ran one test and
returned exit 101 with the expected assertion failure:

| Selector | First observed failure | Later execution in that test process |
|---|---|---|
| `monitor::procs_view::tests::flat_distinct_keys_follow_direction` | `Name desc=false`: observed `[42,17,3]`, expected `[3,17,42]` at `procs_view.rs:342` | First Name panic stopped later loop cases. |
| `monitor::procs_view::tests::tree_roots_and_siblings_follow_name_and_pid_direction` | `Name desc=false`: observed `[42,17,3,10,11]`, expected `[10,11,42,3,17]` at `procs_view.rs:386` | First Name panic stopped later PID loop cases. |
| `monitor::tests::process_sort_keys_render_both_directions_and_keep_confirmation_identity` | `Name desc=true`: observed `[3,17,42]`, expected `[42,17,3]` at `monitor_tests.rs:501` | c/m CPU/RSS cases ran; later Name/PID and confirmation block did not run. |

The flat selector's CPU/RSS cases pass before Name. The tree selector starts
with Name, so no CPU/RSS prelude is claimed there. The actual monitor selector
runs c/m before the failing n/Name case. These are fixture tests: no live
process sampling or signal dispatch occurs.

The raw receipt's `expected_first_failures` field retains wording from before
